229-/// The deepest an expression may nest before the parser gives up with a
230-/// spanned [`Error::Parser`].
231-///
232-/// Every nested bracket, prefix operator, call argument and left-associative
233-/// binary chain makes the expression tree one level deeper. Parsing recurses
234-/// per level and dropping the finished tree recurses per level, so an
235-/// unbounded depth turns a long line of source into "has overflowed its stack"
236-/// and a core dump instead of a diagnostic.
237-///
238-/// The value is deliberately small. libstd gives a spawned thread a 2 MiB
239-/// stack, and a debug build spends roughly 16 KiB of stack per level of nested
240-/// brackets (eleven frames of `parse_primary` .. `parse_postfix`). Measured on
241-/// this repository, 123 levels is where a 2 MiB test thread dies; 64 leaves
242-/// nearly a 2x margin while staying far above anything hand-written.
243-pub const MAX_NESTING_DEPTH: usize = 64;
244-
245-/// The deepest `if` / `for` / `to` / `object` / `try` / `test` blocks may nest
246-/// before the parser gives up with a spanned [`Error::Parser`].
247-///
248-/// A block body is parsed by a recursive call back into `parse_statement`, so
249-/// each nested block is a level of parser recursion, and the analyzer and VM
250-/// then walk the same tree. The same 64-level budget as
251-/// [`MAX_NESTING_DEPTH`] is used, because the same 2 MiB stack has to hold it.
252-pub const MAX_BLOCK_DEPTH: usize = 64;
